Not every bunch of tabs I keep open are purely educational.
A lot of the time, they are basically just shopping lists.
For most things I want to buy, I just put them in a real shopping list,
but the things I leave tabs open for tend to be shopping decisions that I have put a huge amount of thought into.
In a sense, they aren't so much decisions as they are paper-trails for how I landed on a decision.

One of my more recent research sprees has been about screwdrivers,
and it all started when YouTube recommended me a video from Todd Parker [[1]]($link.ref('References')) talking about a bit driver from Wiha called the Centrofix.

I've been using multi-bit screwdrivers for ever now,
so the drivers I'm used to have long shafts with bulky and wobbly magnetic holders at the end to hold stubby bits.
The video about the Centrofix sold me almost imediately on the fact that the driver's collet is so close to the handle rather than at the end of a long shaft.
Then with the use of very long bladed bits, the tool comes to essentially the same length as a standard screwdriver.
I hadn't heard of the "Bit Driver" category of tools before, so I didn't know that this "shaftless" design was actually quite common.

To me, this is the killer feature of bit drivers over the typical multi-bit screwdrivers.
The primary situations where I'm using screwdrivers are in electronics,
and it's quite common to run into screw holes that are recessed in a hole that my multi-bit driver's shaft can't fit into,
so I need to pull out a standard fixed-blade screwdriver, or even the precision screwdriver set.

By now I had thrown the Wiha Centrofix right into my Amazon wishlist.

## [Bits]($section.id('Bits'))

Of course, my collection of screwdriver bits are almost entirely the usual short kind,
and if I wanted to get a bitholder like the Centrofix,
I'll also need to build a solid collection of bits 90mm long or longer.

I don't need every drive style or size out there, just the stuff I use most often.

Tentative Requirements:
- Tamper Resistant Torx
  - My current favorite drive style. I would like to have these more than any other drive style.
  - Mandatory: From T8 to T40
  - Would be nice: T5 and T6

- Internal Hex
  - Tamper resistant is a bonus (because I'm a gear head), but absolutely not necessary.
  - Mandatory: 2mm to 5mm
  - Would be nice: 1.5mm for the occasional stepper motor set screw (they usually come with keys anyways)

- Philips
  - Mandatory: PH2, PH3

In that same Todd Parker Video [[1]]($link.ref('References')),
he mentions that Wiha themselves have a lineup of long-bladed bits that fit perfectly with the Centrofix called PowerBlade,
so thats the first logical place to look.

